<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Encomienda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The encomienda was a system in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> by which the crown granted colonists the right to extract labour and tribute from groups of indigenous people, in return for protecting them and instructing them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. In practice it led to severe abuse, prompting criticism by figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> and attempts at reform through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/encomienda.jpg" alt="Encomienda" width="268" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trabajo propio. Reproducción de un dibujo de Felipe Guamán Poma de Ayala. (Own work. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Hernandez_Giron_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word encomienda comes from the Spanish verb encomendar, to entrust. The institution began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> in the Christian reconquista of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where adelantados were given the right to extract tribute from Muslims and other peasants in areas they conquered and resettled. It resembled a feudal relationship, with military protection traded for tribute or work, and was especially common among the military orders that guarded frontier land. It rewarded those who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, who were exiled in 1492 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada-war">Granada War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1501 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> declared Native Americans subjects of the Crown and so legally equal to Castilians, which made enslaving them illegal except in narrow cases and allowed encomiendas, a bond reserved for full subjects. The Crown began formally granting encomiendas to conquistadors and officials in 1503, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a> set up the system on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1502.</p><p>A grant gave an encomendero a number of people from a community, and Indigenous leaders had to mobilise the tribute and labour, paid in metals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, wheat and pork. In return the encomendero was to give instruction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and Spanish and protect the people. Natives did not lose their land, which stayed with their communities. In practice conditions often resembled forced labour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and some encomenderos used extreme punishment and killing against resistance.</p><p>Grants were first held in perpetuity. Some women and some Indigenous elites held them: Cortés gave two of Moctezuma's daughters extensive encomiendas, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rulers appointed after the conquest sought them. The system was used in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> economies and later in the mining economy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, where it lasted from the 1530s to the eighteenth century. In 1594 Philip II extended it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, with grants to local nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Controversy began early. In 1510 an encomendero named Valenzuela murdered Indigenous leaders who had come to peace talks, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> cacique Enriquillo rebelled from 1519 to 1533. The Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> of 1512-13 tried to regulate treatment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, a priest and former encomendero, spent his life lobbying to abolish the system, which he thought enslaved the native people.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a> limited grants to two generations, gradually ended the system and repeated the ban on enslaving Native Americans. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> tried to enforce them and was killed by rebellious encomenderos, while in Mexico Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> declined to apply them, saying he obeyed but did not comply.</p><p>After the mid-sixteenth century the Crown-managed repartimiento generally replaced the encomienda: native workers were allotted to settlers for a set period, usually several weeks, in an attempt to reduce abuses. The encomienda was abolished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1789.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Encomienda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Spanish rule the Muisca were divided into <b>encomiendas</b>, their numbers fell under epidemics and forced labour, and their language died out in the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Indigenous communities nevertheless survived as self-governing towns that paid tribute and supplied labour, first through the <b>encomienda</b> and later the repartimiento.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>encomienda</b> system extracted labour and tribute from indigenous populations, while the hacienda later emerged as the dominant form of landholding.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With no gold or silver, and a native population too reduced by disease and slave raids for an <b>encomienda</b> system of forced farm labour, Spanish Florida had no large plantations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Law of Wombs made the children of slaves free, any slave entering the territory was freed, the mit'a, <b>encomienda</b> and yanaconazgo were abolished and Indigenous people declared citizens with equal rights, noble ranks disappeared, and the wealthy paid 500 pesos a year to finance the state and army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>To impose royal authority, Charles V issued the New Laws of 1542, which created the viceroyalty with its seat at Lima and restricted the <b>encomiendas</b>, the grants of indigenous tribute and labour held by the conquerors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
